{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And when men strive… and he does not die.</b> One must know why it was necessary to say <b>and he does not die</b>, and not rely on understanding that the verse speaks even without 'he does not die' — whether from what preceded in the laws of murder, or from what it concludes by saying ״אִם יָקוּם וְהִתְהַלֵּךְ״ etc.?" "<b>Indeed</b>, the intent is to say that there is no obligation to give [compensation for] cessation [of work] and healing unless he does not die; but if he dies, he does not give [compensation for] cessation and healing. Therefore Scripture did not say ״שִׁבְתּוֹ יִתֵּן״ etc. until after he arose from his injury and walked about." "And I have a doubt: if they assessed him for death and he did not die — are we to say that a miracle was performed for him so that he did not die, as they said of blessed memory (Ḥulin 42a) regarding a treifah that cannot live, and if it lived a miracle was performed for it (Ḥulin 43a) — so too in the case before us here, if he did not die, this is a miracle, and the obligation of cessation and healing, since it lapsed, lapsed? Or perhaps since he did not die, he is liable for cessation, and from the words of the Rambam in chapter 4 it implies that anyone who did not die, even though they assessed him for death, is liable for cessation and healing." "Further, I found that they said in the Mekhilta, and this is their language: ״וְלֹא יָמוּת וְנָפַל לְמִשְׁכָּב״ — this teaches that if the striker struck a blow sufficient to kill, he is exempt from [paying for] cessation and healing; end quote. This implies even if he did not die, since it hangs the reasoning on the blow and did not say ״the case is if he dies.״" "<b>And it appears</b> that the intent of the Tanna is to say that one is exempt from [paying for] cessation only if he died by means of a blow sufficient to kill; but if he died from a blow not sufficient to kill — in which case the striker is exempt from [the death] penalty — he is liable to give [compensation for] cessation and healing. If he did not give him during his lifetime the [compensation for] his livelihood for some reason, he is liable to give to his heirs everything that would have pertained to their father if he had lived."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:58.480950334Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}